# The guest's memory, kept small so the host gets back what the guest is not using.
#
# Compressed swap: fstab.dsa declares a zram device of half the guest's memory. Idle apps'
# pages are kept there compressed (about a third of their size) instead of resident, and the
# memory they leave is free for page reporting to hand back to Hyper-V. When the guest itself
# runs short, the kernel takes clean page cache before it compresses app memory: swappiness 60,
# the kernel's own default, weighs a file page as cheaper to take than an anonymous one. At 100
# the two cost the same, and with the host short the guest had 552 MiB of the open apps' memory
# in zram beside 686 MiB of page cache; every swapped page an app touches costs a fault and a
# decompression, and the copy in zram stays the VM's memory, while a clean file page that goes
# frees its whole page and costs one read only if it is used again. The daemon's reclaims follow
# the same order (dsa-channeld.rc). Reading one swapped page reads only that page: zram has no seek to save by reading ahead, and
# the neighbours would be resident again for nothing.
#
# Free page reporting hands back only whole free blocks of its order. Where the host takes back
# only 2 MiB or 128 KiB blocks (the host then puts androidboot.dsa.memory_compaction=on on the
# command line), memory an app frees is scattered in small pieces among pages still in use,
# and until they gather into whole blocks none of it goes back. The kernel compacts in the
# background whenever free memory is more fragmented than this setting allows; 40, twice its
# default, has it gather free pages sooner, so more of them reach the host without waiting for
# the control daemon's reclaim. Where the host takes back every free 4 KiB page (order 0, on
# recent Windows, Linux and macOS), background compaction is off: every free page goes back as
# it is, and compaction would only copy pages into free pages the host had taken back, which
# it then backs again. The kernel still compacts when an allocation needs contiguous memory.
#
# zram compresses with LZ4, fast enough for every swap. zstd, its second compressor, compresses
# what stays in zram again during the idle reclaim (dsa-channeld.rc), about a fifth smaller. It
# must be named before swapon_all sizes the device; a kernel without multi-compression refuses
# the write, and zram then keeps LZ4 alone.
#
# What stays cold in zram for long goes on to its backing file on the data disk (fstab.dsa),
# which costs the host disk space instead of memory. Writing wears the user's disk, so zram
# writes nothing until dsa-channeld hands it a budget: with the limit on and no budget, a
# writeback writes nothing. The daemon writes back only while no app is open, only pages no one
# has used for vendor.dsa.memory.writeback_age seconds, and within a daily budget
# (dsa-channeld.rc). The boot can set both times (androidboot.dsa.zram_writeback_after and
# androidboot.dsa.zram_writeback_age), for a check that cannot wait for the defaults.
#
# The kernel's caches of file names and inodes (dentries and inodes, in slab) are weighed
# against page cache when memory is reclaimed. At 200, twice the default, each reclaim, the
# working set trim's included, takes them back twice as readily: a desktop guest walks its
# APEX packages and app folders at boot and seldom again, and a name looked up again is read
# from the page cache or the disk in microseconds. The idle reclaim drops them all anyway.
#
# Each CPU keeps lists of free pages of its own, to hand out without taking the zone's lock.
# Pages on them count as neither free nor used (/proc/meminfo leaves them out of MemFree), free
# page reporting never offers them to the host, and by default the kernel lets the lists grow
# from each zone's low watermark up to an eighth of its memory, shared by the CPUs. Android
# raises that watermark for the display (extra_free_kbytes, about 95 MiB for a 2880x2880 one),
# so the lists of a 2.5 GiB guest may hold 100 to 300 MiB of free memory that stays with the
# VM. A fraction this large keeps each list at its least, four batches (about 1 MiB a CPU and
# zone); a desktop guest's few CPUs rarely contend for the zone's lock.
on boot
    write /sys/devices/virtual/block/zram0/recomp_algorithm "algo=zstd priority=1"
    swapon_all /vendor/etc/fstab.dsa
    write /sys/devices/virtual/block/zram0/writeback_limit_enable 1
    setprop vendor.dsa.memory.writeback_after ${ro.boot.dsa.zram_writeback_after:-600}
    setprop vendor.dsa.memory.writeback_age ${ro.boot.dsa.zram_writeback_age:-1800}
    write /proc/sys/vm/swappiness 60
    write /proc/sys/vm/page-cluster 0
    write /proc/sys/vm/compaction_proactiveness 0
    write /proc/sys/vm/vfs_cache_pressure 200
    write /proc/sys/vm/percpu_pagelist_high_fraction 1048576

# After the action above: both run on boot, in the order they are written.
on boot && property:ro.boot.dsa.memory_compaction=on
    write /proc/sys/vm/compaction_proactiveness 40

# The apps that see the computer's memory as the device's (Google Play: the Play Store and Play
# services). Google Play gives a device under 8 GB a build of Chrome whose browser is 32-bit,
# which this 64-bit only guest cannot start, and the VM's size says nothing about the computer:
# it starts small and grows. The host passes the computer's memory at each start
# (kernel.dsa_host_memory.total_kb in the bootconfig). dsa-channeld names the apps' uids from the
# package manager's list in vendor.dsa.host_memory.uids ("none" for no app), and init hands them
# to the kernel, which then shows those apps the computer's memory as MemTotal in /proc/meminfo
# and in sysinfo(). The framework reads the same two kernel parameters to answer those apps'
# ActivityManager.getMemoryInfo. Only init may write the list (sepolicy/memory.cil), so no app
# can name itself; every other process sees the VM's memory, so lmkd is unchanged.
on property:vendor.dsa.host_memory.uids=*
    write /sys/module/dsa_host_memory/parameters/uids ${vendor.dsa.host_memory.uids}
